course: dictionary
lesson: 6557
title: One short word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some short words have more than one meaning. The letters NI can mean different things, so the context tells you which one. Look at the capital letters, at the place or money words near it, and at the whole sentence.
explain: On a map or in an address, «Belfast, NI» means Northern Ireland, a part of the United Kingdom. On a payslip, «NI number» means National Insurance, the money taken from wages to pay for state pensions and benefits. In science, the symbol Ni is the metal nickel.
explain: Now read a sentence from another language: «u tebe ye kit?» means «do you have a cat?». The answer can be «tak» or «ni», and here «ni» means no. Check by putting your meaning in: «Do you have a cat? No.» It makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: NI can be short for Northern Ireland or National Insurance, and Ni is the chemical symbol for nickel.
fact: Capital letters often show an abbreviation, but a lowercase «ni» in a sentence may be a word from another language.
fact: In the old film «Monty Python and the Holy Grail», the Knights Who Say «Ni!» shout it as a silly word.
fact: In «u tebe ye kit?», the answer «ni» means no, while «tak» means yes.
fact: «spysAty» means to copy someone's answers, which is cheating. A friend may answer «ni, sam roby!», meaning no, do it yourself.
exercise: On a UK payslip, «NI number» is short for what? | Northern Ireland | No Interest | *National Insurance | Nickel
exercise: In «u tebe ye kit?», the answer «ni» means what? | *No | Thank you | Yes | A cat
exercise: In the periodic table, the symbol Ni stands for which metal? | *Nickel | Copper | Iron | Gold
test: A friend asks to copy your answers and you reply «ni, sam roby!». What does your friend mean? | *No, do it yourself | Yes, copy them | Please wait a moment | Thank you for asking
test: «Belfast, NI» is on a map. What does NI stand for here? | New Italy | *Northern Ireland | Nickel | National Insurance
exam: Why do you need context to understand NI? | Because NI is only ever a silly word from a film | Because NI always means Northern Ireland | Because context is only for long words | *Because NI can mean different things, like a place, money, a metal or a word in another language
